n Friday because we need to go  fix this problem and I also want to  stress again that I didn't tell alloy  here's a ridiculous sequence of 13 steps  what happens I always figured it out on  its own it figured out that if this  these particular 13 steps were to happen  we would get a flaw so that was one fun  experience I had in previous company  with alloy here's another one so the the  company this time had been working on a  brand new API for a year it put a ton of  effort into it and very shortly five or  six different systems around the company  we're gonna start consuming this API and  I was brought in to help have the first  system integrate with the API great no  problem there the issue was the API was  actually kind of hard  understand through no fault of the API  itself but it was modeling a lot of  pretty intense it had a lot of pretty  intense business logic behind the scenes  and you couldn't really understand how  to use the API unless you understood the  business logic so what did I do to help  myself understand the business logic I  decided to write an alloy model not to  prove anything wrong or prove anything  right but remember what I said before  writing things down helps you think  better alloy helps me think better and  what did this process look like so  that's me on the left I'm Jay again the  developer I spent most of the time  talking to his name's Bob great guy so I  took the documentation for the API and I  built an initial alloy model and while  trying to build that model I realized  there was gaps in the documentation  because I had to write the alloy piece  and there was no corresponding  documentation to tell me what to do so  I'd go to Bob and I'd say hey how does  ABC work what do I do here and he would  always give me an incredibly detailed  very well thought-out answer awesome so  that would give me more information to  build out my model so I'd go iterate  iterate iterate work on it for a little  bit then I would start to generate